-- The top-200 Egyptian dishes, as recipes.
--
-- 0017 seeded 17 composite dishes. The blueprint's kill line is photo accuracy
-- above 85% on the top 200, and the meal analyser and the plan generator both
-- resolve foods through this graph, so a dish that is not here cannot be read
-- or planned whatever the model sees. This migration takes the graph past 200.
--
-- The rule from 0017 holds and is the whole design: NOT ONE NUTRIENT VALUE IS
-- TYPED HERE. Every dish is a recipe — a list of raw ingredients with gram
-- weights for one household portion (the plate, the sandwich, the bowl the app
-- already shows) and a yield factor for what cooking does. Per-100g nutrition
-- is derived by qamar_nutrients_per_100g from the ingredients' USDA values, so
-- it is only as good as the ingredient coverage and the proportions, and both
-- of those are checkable in a way a remembered calorie figure never is.
--
-- Three consequences of that rule shape what is below:
--
--   1. Ingredients a dish needs that the graph lacked are added as plain foods
--      with a USDA-shaped English name and state, so ingest_usda.ts resolves
--      them like every other ingredient. Water and salt are among them: a soup
--      that omits its water is per-100g fiction, and sodium is the nutrient
--      most Egyptian dishes overshoot.
--
--   2. Composites that 0017 seeded as plain foods and USDA cannot carry —
--      basbousa, konafa, om ali, karkade, sobia — become recipes here rather
--      than waiting for a hand-typed row that would never come.
--
--   3. A sandwich contains a dish (taameya inside its bread), so the derivation
--      learns to follow one recipe into another, and refuses to answer at all
--      when an ingredient has no energy value, instead of quietly undercounting.
--
-- Everything derived is marked human_reviewed = false at confidence 0.5. The
-- proportions are the only estimated part, they are listed as gram weights a
-- nutritionist can read in one sitting, and the household_note flags the ones
-- most in need of that reading.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. Ingredients the new recipes need
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- name_en + food_state is the query ingest_usda.ts sends to FoodData Central,
-- so both are written to overlap the USDA description. A few Egyptian items
-- have no USDA row and stand in for a near relative, noted in the recipe.

